# Scope: ONLY the sysfs_leds type is touched. A generic-sysfs fallback for
# /sys/class/backlight/* was removed deliberately — SELinux cannot scope an
# allow to a path, so granting platform_app write on the generic `sysfs`
# type would open every unlabelled sysfs node on the tree. On the DC-1 the
# amber node is /sys/class/leds/lcd-backlight-amber (verified on-device),
# which is sysfs_leds, so the backlight fallback is dead weight and is
# dropped. Grants are narrowed to platform_app only, which is the domain
# AmberControl actually runs in.
#

typeattribute sysfs_leds mlstrustedobject;

# Gate 3 is DAC: the node ships root:root 0644, so dc1-amber.rc has init
# chmod it; core policy gives init sysfs_leds write but not setattr.
allow init sysfs_leds:file setattr;

# dc1-amber.rc also has init write the Daylight LED driver's own chip-level
# knobs (hwcomposer_disabled_backlight_off_bypass on i2c 2-003c and 5-003c) to
# un-gate the amber string — see the .rc for the gate hypothesis. Those attrs
# live under /sys/bus/i2c/devices/*-003c/, which no genfs_contexts rule labels,
# so they are the generic `sysfs` type rather than sysfs_leds. Core policy is
# not relied on for the write: this repo already found (sepolicy/dc1wlan.te)
# that init lacks generic write on an unlabelled node class on this tree, so
# grant it explicitly. Redundant-if-already-granted is fine — duplicate allows
# are legal, and SELINUX_IGNORE_NEVERALLOWS is set in this GSI.
#
# init is s0 and plain sysfs is already mlstrustedobject, so no MLS gate here.
# This widens init only — never an app domain — and init is already the domain
# that relabels and chmods most of /sys, so no new attack surface is opened.
# The dir/lnk_file perms are for path traversal: /sys/bus/i2c/devices/2-003c is
# a symlink into /sys/devices/platform/..., which init must read to resolve.
# `setattr` is for the second half of the .rc's chip block: it chmods those
# same attrs 0644 so their state can be read back with `adb shell cat` (the
# amber gate is only debuggable if the driver's own view is observable). Same
# grant the sysfs_leds rule above needs, for the same reason.
allow init sysfs:file { open write getattr setattr };
allow init sysfs:dir { search open read getattr };
allow init sysfs:lnk_file { read getattr };

# AmberControl (platform_app) is the only domain that needs to drive the LED
# node. It also inspects the class dirs for discovery.
allow platform_app sysfs_leds:dir { search open read getattr };
allow platform_app sysfs_leds:lnk_file { read getattr };
allow platform_app sysfs_leds:file { getattr open read write append lock map };
